/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Absolute humidity is the mass of water vapour in one cubic metre of air, in grams per cubic metre (g/m³).
   - Relative humidity (RH) is the actual water vapour pressure divided by the saturation vapour pressure at the same temperature, times 100:
   - pv is the actual partial pressure of water vapour in pascals (Pa); psat(T) is the saturation vapour pressure at temperature T in kelvin (K); RH is in percent.
   - Dew point is the temperature at which psat equals pv.
   - These three numbers answer different questions.
   - In hospitals humidity is a treatment variable, not only a comfort figure.
   - A capacitive humidity sensor uses a thin hygroscopic polymer film, such as polyimide, between two electrodes.
   - C is capacitance in farads (F); εr is the relative permittivity (dielectric constant); ε0 = 8.854 × 10−12 F/m; A is electrode area in square metres; d is film thickness in metres.
   - A resistive humidity sensor uses a porous ceramic such as Al2O3 or TiO2.
   - Both types use AC excitation, typically about 1 kHz.
*/
