/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A metal has about 10²⁹ free electrons per m³, fixed.
   - Conductivity is σ = q(nμn + pμp), where σ is in S/m, q = 1.6 × 10−19 C, n and p are electron and hole densities per m³, and μn, μp are mobilities in m²/(V·s).
   - The second readout is a barrier: a p-n diode, Schottky diode or MOS capacitor.
   - In doped silicon at room temperature the carrier density is fixed by the doping, so resistance drifts only about +0.1 to +0.3 %/K, mainly because mobility falls as T rises.
   - Silicon is the default: cheap, single-crystal, and micromachined into diaphragms and beams (MEMS micro sensors).
   - Silicon carbide (SiC, gap 3.2 eV) and gallium nitride (GaN, gap 3.4 eV) keep both effects small to several hundred degrees Celsius.
   - Metal oxides are the gas and humidity family: SnO₂, ZnO, TiO₂, ZrO₂, Ga₂O₃.
   - GaAs and InP give fast, low-noise and infrared devices: GaAs Hall plates, InGaAs photodiodes for 1300–1550 nm.
   - Medicine uses each family.
   - A surface reaction happens at the surface, but the signal comes from the volume.
*/
